<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, Stanley introduced the chromatic symmetric function of a graph, as a symmetric function generalization of the chromatic polynomial of a graph. In this paper, by taking the inverse approach, we associate a polynomial, called the <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">characteristic polynomial</em>, to each symmetric function. Although the polynomials we consider are not the chromatic polynomials of graphs in general, those obtained from geometric contexts appear to share one of the most interesting features of chromatic polynomials, namely the <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">log-concavity</em> (cf. §<a href="#S2" title="2. Two-dimensional log-concavity ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>Chromatic polynomial and characteristic polynomial</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">chromatic polynomial</em> of a graph <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{X}_{\Gamma}(m)\in\mathbb{Q}[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒳</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{X}_{\Gamma}(m)\in\mathbb{Q}[m]</annotation></semantics></math> such that for each <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}\in\mathbb{N}=\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}\in\mathbb{N}=\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the value <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{X}_{\Gamma}(m_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒳</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{X}_{\Gamma}(m_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> equals the number of proper colorings of the vertices of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> colors. Here, a coloring is called <em id="S1.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">proper</em> if any two adjacent vertices (that is, vertices connected by an edge) take different colors. In this paper, we regard <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{X}_{\Gamma}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒳</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{X}_{\Gamma}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> as a polynomial in the variable <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, and write <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{X}_{\Gamma}(m_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒳</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{X}_{\Gamma}(m_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> for its evaluation at a positive integer <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">chromatic symmetric function</em> <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{csf}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖼𝗌𝖿</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{csf}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathsf{csf}_{\Gamma}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}\kappa:V(\Gamma)\to\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1},\\
\mathrm{proper}\end{subarray}}\prod_{v\in V(\Gamma)}x_{\kappa(v)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖼𝗌𝖿</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>proper</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{csf}_{\Gamma}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}\kappa:V(\Gamma)\to\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1},\\
\mathrm{proper}\end{subarray}}\prod_{v\in V(\Gamma)}x_{\kappa(v)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{i}\}_{i\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{i}\}_{i\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}}</annotation></semantics></math> are formal variables and <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of vertices of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. This is obviously a symmetric function in <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the substitution</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}=x_{2}=\cdots=x_{m_{0}}=1x_{m_{0}+1}=x_{m_{0}+2}=\cdots=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}=x_{2}=\cdots=x_{m_{0}}=1x_{m_{0}+1}=x_{m_{0}+2}=\cdots=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">to <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{csf}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖼𝗌𝖿</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{csf}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> gives the integer <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{X}_{\Gamma}(m_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒳</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{X}_{\Gamma}(m_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. In this sense, <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{csf}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖼𝗌𝖿</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{csf}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> can be considered as a generalization of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{X}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒳</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{X}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the log-concavity proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> appears only after the substitution (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1.1. Chromatic polynomial and characteristic polynomial ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) and hence it makes sense to extend the chromatic polynomials to more general contexts when one is interested in the log-concavity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we first observe that the process of producing a polynomial out of a symmetric function by the substitution (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1.1. Chromatic polynomial and characteristic polynomial ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) works not only for chromatic symmetric functions of graphs but also for all symmetric functions.
Indeed, the substitution (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1.1. Chromatic polynomial and characteristic polynomial ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) produces a map <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{S}_{F}:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{S}_{F}:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> for any symmetric function <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> in the variables <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with rational coefficients.
It is straightforward to check that <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{S}_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{S}_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial function whose associated polynomial in <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}[m]</annotation></semantics></math> is called the <em id="S1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">characteristic polynomial</em> <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{S}_{F}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{S}_{F}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> (Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 3.1. ‣ 3.2. Characteristic polynomial of a symmetric function ‣ 3. Characteristic polynomials of symmetric functions and representations ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">It turns out that this definition is quite natural. To explain this, we first write it in a different but equivalent form.
Let <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> be the power series ring of the symmetric functions in <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Lambda=\lim_{\longleftarrow}\mathbb{Q}\llbracket x_{1},\cdots,x_{n}\rrbracket^{\mathbb{S}_{n}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mo stretchy="false">⟵</mo></munder><mi>ℚ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟦</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟧</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda=\lim_{\longleftarrow}\mathbb{Q}\llbracket x_{1},\cdots,x_{n}\rrbracket^{\mathbb{S}_{n}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and let <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{n}\subset\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{n}\subset\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> be the subspace of elements of degree <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{p}_{n}=\sum_{i\geq 1}x_{i}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝗉</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{p}_{n}=\sum_{i\geq 1}x_{i}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>th power sum symmetric function for <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{p}_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝗉</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{p}_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. It is well-known that <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Lambda\cong\mathbb{Q}\llbracket\mathsf{p}_{1},\mathsf{p}_{2},\cdots\rrbracket" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>≅</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟦</mo><msub><mi>𝗉</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝗉</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">⟧</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\cong\mathbb{Q}\llbracket\mathsf{p}_{1},\mathsf{p}_{2},\cdots\rrbracket</annotation></semantics></math>. The map associating the characteristic polynomial to each symmetric function</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathscr{S}}:\Lambda\longrightarrow\mathbb{Q}[m]\llbracket q\rrbracket,\quad(F\in\Lambda_{n})\mapsto\mathscr{S}_{F}(m)q^{n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟦</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟧</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathscr{S}}:\Lambda\longrightarrow\mathbb{Q}[m]\llbracket q\rrbracket,\quad(F\in\Lambda_{n})\mapsto\mathscr{S}_{F}(m)q^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">is precisely the <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra homomorphism
which sends <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{p}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝗉</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{p}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="mq^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">mq^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is a formal variable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">A remarkable feature of this map is that it behaves nicely with <em id="S1.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">plethysm</em> <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\circ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\circ</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> (see §<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1. Symmetric functions and plethysm ‣ 3. Characteristic polynomials of symmetric functions and representations ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>).
In fact, it converts the plethysm <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\circ G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\circ G</annotation></semantics></math> of symmetric functions <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in\Lambda_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in\Lambda_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\Lambda_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\Lambda_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>
to the composition <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathscr{S}_{F}\circ\mathscr{S}_{G})(m)=\mathscr{S}_{F}(\mathscr{S}_{G}(m))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathscr{S}_{F}\circ\mathscr{S}_{G})(m)=\mathscr{S}_{F}(\mathscr{S}_{G}(m))</annotation></semantics></math>
(Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3.2. Characteristic polynomial of a symmetric function ‣ 3. Characteristic polynomials of symmetric functions and representations ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).
Conversely, this feature uniquely determines <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathscr{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathscr{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>
as a <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra homomorphism <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\to\mathbb{Q}[m]\llbracket q\rrbracket" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟦</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟧</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\to\mathbb{Q}[m]\llbracket q\rrbracket</annotation></semantics></math>
(Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.2. Characteristic polynomial of a symmetric function ‣ 3. Characteristic polynomials of symmetric functions and representations ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We can also define the characteristic polynomial of a symmetric group representation.
Let <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the symmetric group of <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> letters and <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space spanned by irreducible <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-representations. Let</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ch}=\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}:R_{n}\xrightarrow{~\cong~}\Lambda_{n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ch</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ch</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≅</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ch}=\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}:R_{n}\xrightarrow{~\cong~}\Lambda_{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">be the Frobenius characteristic map (cf. (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In 3.4. Characteristic polynomial of an 𝕊_𝑛-representation ‣ 3. Characteristic polynomials of symmetric functions and representations ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.16</span></a>)). The <em id="S1.SS1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">characteristic polynomial</em> of an <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{S}_{V}(m)=\mathscr{S}_{\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}(V)}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><mrow><msub><mi>ch</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{S}_{V}(m)=\mathscr{S}_{\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}(V)}(m)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2. </span>Characteristic polynomials of varieties</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we work out the characteristic polynomials of
examples from geometry, especially the cohomology of varieties
equipped with an action of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
These include the <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-fold products of the projective spaces
(cf. §<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1. 𝑛-fold products ‣ 4. Characteristic polynomials of varieties and log-concavity ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) and the GIT moduli space of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points on <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for odd <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. §<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. GIT moduli space of 𝑛 points on ℙ^1 ‣ 4. Characteristic polynomials of varieties and log-concavity ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>). In both cases, the <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-action that permutes the <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> ordered points induces graded <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-representations on the cohomology. We compute the characteristic polynomials of these representations and establish suitable log-concavity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Another remarkable example is a regular semisimple <em id="S1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Hessenberg variety</em> <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. It is a subvariety in the flag variety of <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined by a function <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:\{1,\cdots,n\}\to\{1,\cdots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:\{1,\cdots,n\}\to\{1,\cdots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(i)\geq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(i)\geq i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(i)\geq h(i-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(i)\geq h(i-1)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
There is a natural action of <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> on its cohomology <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(X_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X_{h})</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> whose characteristic polynomial satisfies</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}H(X_{h})=\mathsf{csf}_{\Gamma_{h}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ch</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝖼𝗌𝖿</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}H(X_{h})=\mathsf{csf}_{\Gamma_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is the involution on <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> that sends <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{p}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝗉</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{p}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)^{n}\mathsf{p}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝗉</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)^{n}\mathsf{p}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is
the incomparability graph associated to <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem13" title="Theorem 4.13. ‣ 4.3. Hessenberg varieties ‣ 4. Characteristic polynomials of varieties and log-concavity ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.13</span></a>). While this equality can be written in a graded version, we stick to this simpler form here.
In particular, applying <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{S}</annotation></semantics></math> to (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1.2. Characteristic polynomials of varieties ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>), we get</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)^{n}\mathscr{S}_{H(X_{h})}(-m)=\mathscr{X}_{\Gamma_{h}}(m)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒳</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)^{n}\mathscr{S}_{H(X_{h})}(-m)=\mathscr{X}_{\Gamma_{h}}(m).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, the characteristic polynomial <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{S}_{H(X_{h})}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{S}_{H(X_{h})}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> of the Hessenberg variety is log-concave. See §<a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Hessenberg varieties ‣ 4. Characteristic polynomials of varieties and log-concavity ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> for further details.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3. </span>Characteristic polynomial of <math id="S1.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main focus of this paper lies on the moduli space <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math> of stable rational curves with <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> marked points.
See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> for basic facts about this moduli space and its cohomology.
This is a case where the characteristic polynomial is not the chromatic polynomial of any graph.
The <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-action on <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math> permuting the <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> marked points gives rise to a graded <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-representation on its cohomology <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently,
we obtain a bivariate polynomial</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{P}_{n}(m,t)=\sum_{k=0}^{n-3}\mathscr{P}_{n,k}(m)t^{k}~\in\mathbb{Q}[m,t],\quad\text{ where }~\mathscr{P}_{n,k}(m)=\mathscr{S}_{\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}H^{2k}(\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n})}(m)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> where </mtext><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><mrow><msub><mi>ch</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{P}_{n}(m,t)=\sum_{k=0}^{n-3}\mathscr{P}_{n,k}(m)t^{k}~\in\mathbb{Q}[m,t],\quad\text{ where }~\mathscr{P}_{n,k}(m)=\mathscr{S}_{\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}H^{2k}(\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n})}(m)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for each <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
We write the generating series of <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{P}=1+mq+\frac{m(m+1)}{2}q^{2}+\sum_{n\geq 3}\mathscr{P}_{n}(m,t)q^{n}~\in\mathbb{Q}[m,t]\llbracket q\rrbracket" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟦</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟧</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{P}=1+mq+\frac{m(m+1)}{2}q^{2}+\sum_{n\geq 3}\mathscr{P}_{n}(m,t)q^{n}~\in\mathbb{Q}[m,t]\llbracket q\rrbracket</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">for a formal parameter <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> recording <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recursive formulas for computing <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> can be deduced from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, where we developed recursive formulas for <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}H^{*}(\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ch</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}H^{*}(\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n})</annotation></semantics></math> that use only arithmetic operations and plethysm. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Theorem 1.3]</cite> or §<a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1. Recursive formulas for the representations ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> and <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. Recursive formulas for characteristic polynomials ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To present explicit formulas, let <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the characteristic polynomial</p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}(m)=\mathscr{S}_{\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}H^{2k}(\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n+1})}(m)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><mrow><msub><mi>ch</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}(m)=\mathscr{S}_{\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}H^{2k}(\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n+1})}(m)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">of the <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-representation on <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2k}(\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2k}(\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n+1})</annotation></semantics></math> induced by the action that permutes the first <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> markings while keeping the last fixed. The advantage of <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}</annotation></semantics></math> is that there is a rather straightforward combinatorial formula</p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}H^{2k}(\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n+1})=\sum_{T\in\mathscr{T}_{n,k}}\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}(U_{T})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ch</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>ch</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}H^{2k}(\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n+1})=\sum_{T\in\mathscr{T}_{n,k}}\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}(U_{T})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">for the Frobenius characteristic
in terms of <em id="S1.SS3.p3.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">weighted rooted trees</em> <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and the associated permutation representations <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, Proposition 5.12]</cite>. In particular, we have</p>
<table id="S1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}=\sum_{T\in\mathscr{T}_{n,k}}\mathscr{S}_{\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}(U_{T})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><mrow><msub><mi>ch</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}=\sum_{T\in\mathscr{T}_{n,k}}\mathscr{S}_{\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}(U_{T})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.4" class="ltx_p">For precise definitions of <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{n,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{n,k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, we refer the reader to §<a href="#S5" title="5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We define the generating series of <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}=1+mq+\sum_{n\geq 2}\mathscr{Q}_{n}(m,t)q^{n}~\in\mathbb{Q}[m,t]\llbracket q\rrbracket,\quad\text{ where }~\mathscr{Q}_{n}(m,t)=\sum_{k=0}^{n-2}\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}(m)t^{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟦</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟧</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> where </mtext><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}=1+mq+\sum_{n\geq 2}\mathscr{Q}_{n}(m,t)q^{n}~\in\mathbb{Q}[m,t]\llbracket q\rrbracket,\quad\text{ where }~\mathscr{Q}_{n}(m,t)=\sum_{k=0}^{n-2}\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}(m)t^{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Moreover, we consider a partial sum</p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}^{+}=\sum_{T\in\mathscr{T}_{n,k}^{+}}\mathscr{S}_{\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}(U_{T})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><mrow><msub><mi>ch</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}^{+}=\sum_{T\in\mathscr{T}_{n,k}^{+}}\mathscr{S}_{\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}(U_{T})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">of (<a href="#S1.E4" title="In 1.3. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>)
where <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{n,k}^{+}\subset\mathscr{T}_{n,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{n,k}^{+}\subset\mathscr{T}_{n,k}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of weighted rooted trees having positive weights at the root vertex. The generating series for <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}^{+}=mq+\sum_{n\geq 2}\mathscr{Q}_{n}^{+}q^{n}~\in\mathbb{Q}[m,t]\llbracket q\rrbracket,\quad\text{ where }~\mathscr{Q}_{n}^{+}=\sum_{k=0}^{n-2}\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}^{+}t^{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟦</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟧</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> where </mtext><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}^{+}=mq+\sum_{n\geq 2}\mathscr{Q}_{n}^{+}q^{n}~\in\mathbb{Q}[m,t]\llbracket q\rrbracket,\quad\text{ where }~\mathscr{Q}_{n}^{+}=\sum_{k=0}^{n-2}\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}^{+}t^{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.4" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{P}</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed from <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed from <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed by its own recursion as follows (cf. §<a href="#S5" title="5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem11" title="Theorem 5.11. ‣ 5.2. Recursive formulas for characteristic polynomials ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11</span></a>)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{P}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is determined by the following.</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{P}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are related by the formula</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+t)\mathscr{P}=(1+t+mqt)\mathscr{Q}-\frac{t}{2}(\mathscr{Q}^{2}-\mathscr{Q}^{[2]})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+t)\mathscr{P}=(1+t+mqt)\mathscr{Q}-\frac{t}{2}(\mathscr{Q}^{2}-\mathscr{Q}^{[2]}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}^{[2]}(m,q,t)=\mathscr{Q}(m,q^{2},t^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}^{[2]}(m,q,t)=\mathscr{Q}(m,q^{2},t^{2})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the plethystic exponential of </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">: </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}=\Exp(\mathscr{Q}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}=\Exp(\mathscr{Q}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> satisfies the following two equivalent formulas.</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathrm{(Recursive)}\quad&amp;\mathscr{Q}^{+}=mq+\sum_{r\geq 3}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{r-2}t^{i}\right)(\mathsf{h}_{r}\circ\mathscr{Q}^{+}),\\
\mathrm{(Exponential)}\quad&amp;\Exp(t\mathscr{Q}^{+})=t^{2}\Exp(\mathscr{Q}^{+})+(1-t)(1+t+mqt).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Recursive</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝗁</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Exponential</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathrm{(Recursive)}\quad&amp;\mathscr{Q}^{+}=mq+\sum_{r\geq 3}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{r-2}t^{i}\right)(\mathsf{h}_{r}\circ\mathscr{Q}^{+}),\\
\mathrm{(Exponential)}\quad&amp;\Exp(t\mathscr{Q}^{+})=t^{2}\Exp(\mathscr{Q}^{+})+(1-t)(1+t+mqt).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">An equivalent polynomial version of this theorem is the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem12" title="Corollary 5.12. ‣ 5.2. Recursive formulas for characteristic polynomials ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.12</span></a>)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are related by</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+t)\mathscr{P}_{n}=\mathscr{Q}_{n}-\frac{1}{2}t\left(\sum_{h=2}^{n-2}\mathscr{Q}_{h}\mathscr{Q}_{n-h}-\mathscr{Q}_{\frac{n}{2}}^{[2]}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+t)\mathscr{P}_{n}=\mathscr{Q}_{n}-\frac{1}{2}t\left(\sum_{h=2}^{n-2}\mathscr{Q}_{h}\mathscr{Q}_{n-h}-\mathscr{Q}_{\frac{n}{2}}^{[2]}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{\frac{n}{2}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{\frac{n}{2}}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> odd, and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{\frac{n}{2}}^{[2]}(m,t)=\mathscr{Q}_{\frac{n}{2}}(m,t^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{\frac{n}{2}}^{[2]}(m,t)=\mathscr{Q}_{\frac{n}{2}}(m,t^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Set <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{1}^{+}=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{1}^{+}=m</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathscr{Q}_{n}^{+}&amp;=\sum_{\lambda\vdash n}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{\ell(\lambda)-2}t^{i}\right)\prod_{j=1}^{m}\left(\mathsf{h}_{r_{j}}\circ\mathscr{Q}_{n_{j}}^{+}\right)\mathscr{Q}_{n}=\sum_{\lambda\vdash n}\prod_{j=1}^{m}\left(\mathsf{h}_{r_{j}}\circ\mathscr{Q}_{n_{j}}^{+}\right)\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⊢</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝗁</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⊢</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝗁</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathscr{Q}_{n}^{+}&amp;=\sum_{\lambda\vdash n}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{\ell(\lambda)-2}t^{i}\right)\prod_{j=1}^{m}\left(\mathsf{h}_{r_{j}}\circ\mathscr{Q}_{n_{j}}^{+}\right)\mathscr{Q}_{n}=\sum_{\lambda\vdash n}\prod_{j=1}^{m}\left(\mathsf{h}_{r_{j}}\circ\mathscr{Q}_{n_{j}}^{+}\right)\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the parts of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> with multiplicities <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=(n_{1}^{r_{1}},\cdots,n_{s}^{r_{s}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=(n_{1}^{r_{1}},\cdots,n_{s}^{r_{s}})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}&gt;\cdots&gt;n_{s}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}&gt;\cdots&gt;n_{s}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\lambda)=\sum_{j=1}^{s}r_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\lambda)=\sum_{j=1}^{s}r_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">By using the above formulas, we implemented a computer program to explicitly compute the characteristic polynomials, and carried out computations for <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 72" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>72</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 72</annotation></semantics></math>. The computational results allow us to investigate the log-concavity properties of the characteristic polynomials. Since <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are bivariate polynomials, various types of log-concavity can be considered (cf. §<a href="#S2" title="2. Two-dimensional log-concavity ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), and our computations indicate that they exhibit several such properties. Based on this numerical evidence, we propose a conjecture concerning the log-concavity of the characteristic polynomials (Conjecture <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem14" title="Conjecture 5.14. ‣ 5.2. Recursive formulas for characteristic polynomials ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.14</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.4. </span>Asymptotic formulas and log-concavity</h3>

<div id="S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study asymptotic formulas of <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{P}_{n,k}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{P}_{n,k}(m)</annotation></semantics></math>, as <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> grows, in two directions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the values <math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{P}_{n,k}(m_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{P}_{n,k}(m_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> at a given <math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> for given <math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the coefficients of <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{n-j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{n-j}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{P}_{n,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{P}_{n,k}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{P}_{n,k}^{n-j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{P}_{n,k}^{n-j}</annotation></semantics></math>, for given <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j,k\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j,k\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These formulas lead us to corresponding asymptotic log-concavity properties which are far reaching generalizations of the corresponding results in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the asymptotics, we use the constants</p>
<table id="S1.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}=\frac{(k+1)^{k-1}}{k!}d_{k}=\frac{(k+1)^{k-2}}{k!}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}=\frac{(k+1)^{k-1}}{k!}d_{k}=\frac{(k+1)^{k-2}}{k!}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1. ‣ 6.1. Asymptotic formulas ‣ 6. Asymptotic behavior and log-concavity ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then as <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> grows,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}(m_{0})=\frac{c_{k}}{((k+1)m_{0}-1)!}n^{(k+1)m_{0}-1}+o(n^{(k+1)m_{0}-1})~\text{ and}\\
&amp;\mathscr{P}_{n,k}(m_{0})=\frac{d_{k}}{((k+1)m_{0}-1)!}n^{(k+1)m_{0}-1}+o(n^{(k+1)m_{0}-1}).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}(m_{0})=\frac{c_{k}}{((k+1)m_{0}-1)!}n^{(k+1)m_{0}-1}+o(n^{(k+1)m_{0}-1})~\text{ and}\\
&amp;\mathscr{P}_{n,k}(m_{0})=\frac{d_{k}}{((k+1)m_{0}-1)!}n^{(k+1)m_{0}-1}+o(n^{(k+1)m_{0}-1}).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">From this, the following asymptotic log-concavity follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Corollary <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem9" title="Corollary 6.9. ‣ 6.2. Asymptotic log-concavity ‣ 6. Asymptotic behavior and log-concavity ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.9</span></a>)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0},k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0},k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;\mathscr{P}_{n,k}(m_{0})^{2}\geq\mathscr{P}_{n,k-1}(m_{0})\mathscr{P}_{n,k+1}(m_{0})~\text{ and}\\
&amp;\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}(m_{0})^{2}\geq\mathscr{Q}_{n,k-1}(m_{0})\mathscr{Q}_{n,k+1}(m_{0})\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and</mtext></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;\mathscr{P}_{n,k}(m_{0})^{2}\geq\mathscr{P}_{n,k-1}(m_{0})\mathscr{P}_{n,k+1}(m_{0})~\text{ and}\\
&amp;\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}(m_{0})^{2}\geq\mathscr{Q}_{n,k-1}(m_{0})\mathscr{Q}_{n,k+1}(m_{0})\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for sufficiently large <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, when <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the inequalities are strict.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3 (Theorem ). ‣ 1.4. Asymptotic formulas and log-concavity ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> and Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 1.4 (Corollary ). ‣ 1.4. Asymptotic formulas and log-concavity ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>
generalize the asymptotic formulas and the asymptotic log-concavity for the Betti numbers of <math id="S1.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n}/\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n}/\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, §6]</cite> which correspond to the case of <math id="S1.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. Remark <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem2" title="Remark 6.2. ‣ 6.1. Asymptotic formulas ‣ 6. Asymptotic behavior and log-concavity ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we investigate the asymptotic behaviors of the coefficients in <math id="S1.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{P}_{n,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{P}_{n,k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that the <em id="S1.SS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">signless Stirling number of the first kind</em> <math id="S1.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(n,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(n,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of <math id="S1.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint cycles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Write
<math id="S1.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{P}_{n,k}(m)=\sum_{j}\mathscr{P}^{j}_{n,k}m^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{P}_{n,k}(m)=\sum_{j}\mathscr{P}^{j}_{n,k}m^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}(m)=\sum_{j}\mathscr{Q}^{j}_{n,k}m^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}(m)=\sum_{j}\mathscr{Q}^{j}_{n,k}m^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6.6. ‣ 6.1. Asymptotic formulas ‣ 6. Asymptotic behavior and log-concavity ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a>)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j,k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j,k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then as <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> grows,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}^{n-j}=c_{k}\cdot\frac{(k+1)^{n-j}c(n,n-j)}{n!}+o\left(\frac{(k+1)^{n}n^{2j}}{n!}\right)~\text{ and}\\
&amp;\mathscr{P}_{n,k}^{n-j}=d_{k}\cdot\frac{(k+1)^{n-j}c(n,n-j)}{n!}+o\left(\frac{(k+1)^{n}n^{2j}}{n!}\right).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}^{n-j}=c_{k}\cdot\frac{(k+1)^{n-j}c(n,n-j)}{n!}+o\left(\frac{(k+1)^{n}n^{2j}}{n!}\right)~\text{ and}\\
&amp;\mathscr{P}_{n,k}^{n-j}=d_{k}\cdot\frac{(k+1)^{n-j}c(n,n-j)}{n!}+o\left(\frac{(k+1)^{n}n^{2j}}{n!}\right).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Based on this, we obtain the following two asymptotic log-concavity results about the coefficients when we vary the powers of <math id="S1.SS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. In fact, in the first case, they are asymptotically <em id="S1.SS4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ultra</em> log-concave.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.6</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Corollary <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem10" title="Corollary 6.10. ‣ 6.2. Asymptotic log-concavity ‣ 6. Asymptotic behavior and log-concavity ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.10</span></a>)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;\left(\frac{\mathscr{P}_{n,k}^{n-j}}{\binom{n-3}{k}}\right)^{2}\geq\left(\frac{\mathscr{P}_{n,k-1}^{n-j}}{\binom{n-3}{k-1}}\right)\left(\frac{\mathscr{P}_{n,k+1}^{n-j}}{\binom{n-3}{k+1}}\right)~\text{ and}\\
