<div id="S5.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Now one can interpret the symmetric function <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}U_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ch</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}U_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> as a symmetric function encoding colorings of the inputs of <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> as follows. For a labeled weighted rooted tree <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of the (labeled) inputs of <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and let</p>
<table id="S5.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{K}_{T}:=\{\kappa:I_{T}\to\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒦</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{K}_{T}:=\{\kappa:I_{T}\to\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">be the set of <em id="S5.SS3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">colorings</em> of the inputs of <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> (by the colors indexed by positive integers).
We consider two colorings to be the same if they are in the same orbit of <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Stab}(T)\subset\mathbb{S}_{I_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Stab</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Stab}(T)\subset\mathbb{S}_{I_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a coloring <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote</p>
<table id="S5.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\kappa}:=\prod_{i\in I_{T}}x_{\kappa(i)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>κ</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\kappa}:=\prod_{i\in I_{T}}x_{\kappa(i)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.18</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem18.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem18.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathscr{T}_{n,k}^{\mathrm{lab}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>lab</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathscr{T}_{n,k}^{\mathrm{lab}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S5.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5.7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}{U_{T}}=\sum_{\kappa\in\mathscr{K}_{T}}x^{\kappa}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ch</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒦</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>κ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}{U_{T}}=\sum_{\kappa\in\mathscr{K}_{T}}x^{\kappa}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">This is a consequence of Pólya theory. See Theorem 7.24.4 and (7.120) in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.
However, for the reader’s convenience, we provide a proof using Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem16" title="Lemma 5.16. ‣ 5.3. Interpretation as the count of colorings ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.16</span></a> and the induction on the number of vertices of <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">When <math id="S5.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> has only one vertex, the root, then <math id="S5.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}U_{T}=\mathsf{h}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ch</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝗁</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}U_{T}=\mathsf{h}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and (<a href="#S5.E7" title="In Proposition 5.18. ‣ 5.3. Interpretation as the count of colorings ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a>) is immediate.
Suppose that <math id="S5.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert V(T)\rvert&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert V(T)\rvert&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
The identity</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathsf{h}_{r}\circ\sum_{\kappa\in\mathscr{K}_{T}}x^{\kappa}=\sum_{\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\ldots,\kappa_{r}\in\mathscr{K}_{T}}x^{\kappa_{1}}x^{\kappa_{2}}\cdots x^{\kappa_{r}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗁</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">∘</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒦</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>κ</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒦</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{h}_{r}\circ\sum_{\kappa\in\mathscr{K}_{T}}x^{\kappa}=\sum_{\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\ldots,\kappa_{r}\in\mathscr{K}_{T}}x^{\kappa_{1}}x^{\kappa_{2}}\cdots x^{\kappa_{r}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">is immediate from the definition of the plethysm product (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, I.8]</cite>).
Combining this with the decomposition in Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem16" title="Lemma 5.16. ‣ 5.3. Interpretation as the count of colorings ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.16</span></a>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}U_{T}=\left(\sum_{\kappa_{0}\in\mathscr{K}_{T_{0}}}x^{\kappa_{0}}\right)\prod_{j=1}^{l}\sum_{\kappa_{j1},\kappa_{j2},\cdots,\kappa_{jr_{j}}\in\mathscr{K}_{T_{j}}}x^{\kappa_{j1}}x^{\kappa_{j2}}\cdots x^{\kappa_{jr_{j}}}=\sum_{\kappa\in\mathscr{K}_{T}}x^{\kappa}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ch</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒦</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow></munder><msup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>l</mi></munderover><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒦</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></munder><msup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒦</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>κ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}U_{T}=\left(\sum_{\kappa_{0}\in\mathscr{K}_{T_{0}}}x^{\kappa_{0}}\right)\prod_{j=1}^{l}\sum_{\kappa_{j1},\kappa_{j2},\cdots,\kappa_{jr_{j}}\in\mathscr{K}_{T_{j}}}x^{\kappa_{j1}}x^{\kappa_{j2}}\cdots x^{\kappa_{jr_{j}}}=\sum_{\kappa\in\mathscr{K}_{T}}x^{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">where the first equality holds by the induction hypothesis applied to each <math id="S5.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and the second equality holds since the product maps</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{0}\times\prod_{1\leq j\leq l,1\leq i\leq r_{j}}\kappa_{ji}\ :\ I_{T_{0}}\times\prod_{1\leq j\leq l,1\leq i\leq r_{j}}I_{T_{j}}\to\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">×</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.778em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">×</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{0}\times\prod_{1\leq j\leq l,1\leq i\leq r_{j}}\kappa_{ji}\ :\ I_{T_{0}}\times\prod_{1\leq j\leq l,1\leq i\leq r_{j}}I_{T_{j}}\to\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p8.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p8.4.1" class="ltx_text">are precisely the colorings of <math id="S5.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">An analogous statement holds for <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ch</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}T)</annotation></semantics></math> if we
imposes a “properness” condition on colorings of the inputs as follows.
For each vertex <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a unique weighted rooted subtree <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies all the conditions (1)–(4) in Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem16" title="Lemma 5.16. ‣ 5.3. Interpretation as the count of colorings ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.16</span></a> with <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in the place of <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> as its root vertex.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.19</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem19.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathscr{T}_{n,k}^{\mathrm{lab}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>lab</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathscr{T}_{n,k}^{\mathrm{lab}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We call a coloring of the inputs of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S5.Thmtheorem19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">proper</em> if it satisfies the following two conditions:</p>
<ol id="S5.I6" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">if two different inputs are attached to the same vertex, then they are colored by different colors;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">if two disjoint weighted rooted subtrees of the form <math id="S5.I6.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphic by the action of <math id="S5.I6.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Stab}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Stab</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Stab}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> and each subtree has an <em id="S5.I6.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">odd</em> number of inputs, then they are non-isomorphic as colored weighted rooted trees.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We denote by <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{K}^{\prime}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒦</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{K}^{\prime}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>
the set of all proper colorings of the inputs of <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.20</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem20.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem20.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathscr{T}_{n,k}^{\mathrm{lab}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>lab</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathscr{T}_{n,k}^{\mathrm{lab}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,</span></p>
<table id="S5.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5.8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}{U_{T}})=\sum_{\kappa\in\mathscr{K}^{\prime}_{T}}x^{\kappa}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ch</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒦</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></munder><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>κ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(\mathrm{ch}_{\mathbb{S}_{n}}{U_{T}})=\sum_{\kappa\in\mathscr{K}^{\prime}_{T}}x^{\kappa}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof is essentially the same as that of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem18" title="Proposition 5.18. ‣ 5.3. Interpretation as the count of colorings ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.18</span></a>, except that we need an additional identity</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathsf{e}_{r}\circ\sum_{\kappa\in\mathscr{K}^{\prime}_{T}}x^{\kappa}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\ldots,\kappa_{r}\in\mathscr{K}^{\prime}_{T}\\
\text{distinct}\end{subarray}}x^{\kappa_{1}}x^{\kappa_{2}}\cdots x^{\kappa_{r}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖾</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">∘</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒦</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>κ</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒦</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext>distinct</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{e}_{r}\circ\sum_{\kappa\in\mathscr{K}^{\prime}_{T}}x^{\kappa}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\ldots,\kappa_{r}\in\mathscr{K}^{\prime}_{T}\\
\text{distinct}\end{subarray}}x^{\kappa_{1}}x^{\kappa_{2}}\cdots x^{\kappa_{r}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">for any <math id="S5.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and weighted rooted trees <math id="S5.SS3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, which is also immediate from the definition of the plethysm product (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, I.8]</cite>). We omit the details.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">We will call the right hand side of (<a href="#S5.E8" title="In Proposition 5.20. ‣ 5.3. Interpretation as the count of colorings ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>) <em id="S5.SS3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">the chromatic symmetric function</em> of a labeled weighted rooted tree <math id="S5.SS3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, which is clearly a symmetric function by definition.
Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem20" title="Proposition 5.20. ‣ 5.3. Interpretation as the count of colorings ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.20</span></a> shows that the Frobenius characteristic of the <math id="S5.SS3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-dual of the representation <math id="S5.SS3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is an analogue for <math id="S5.SS3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of the chromatic symmetric function of a graph. However, in our case, the coloring is on inputs not on vertices, the properness condition is different from the usual one, and two colorings in the same orbit of <math id="S5.SS3.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Stab}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Stab</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Stab}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> are identified.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p13.1" class="ltx_p">The following is now immediate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.21</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem21.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathscr{T}_{n,k}^{\mathrm{lab}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>lab</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathscr{T}_{n,k}^{\mathrm{lab}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I7" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I7.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{S}_{U_{T}}(m_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{S}_{U_{T}}(m_{0})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I7.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the number of colorings of the inputs of </span><math id="S5.I7.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I7.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> by </span><math id="S5.I7.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I7.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> colors.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I7.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{S}^{\omega}_{U_{T}}(m_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mi>ω</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{S}^{\omega}_{U_{T}}(m_{0})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I7.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the number of proper colorings of the inputs of </span><math id="S5.I7.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I7.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> by </span><math id="S5.I7.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I7.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> colors.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem21.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem21.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{S}_{U_{T}}(m)=(-1)^{n}\mathscr{S}^{\omega}_{U_{T}}(-m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mi>ω</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{S}_{U_{T}}(m)=(-1)^{n}\mathscr{S}^{\omega}_{U_{T}}(-m)</annotation></semantics></math> and it has nonnegative coefficients.  <math id="S5.Thmtheorem21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">(1) and (2) are immediate from Propositions <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem18" title="Proposition 5.18. ‣ 5.3. Interpretation as the count of colorings ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.18</span></a> and <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem20" title="Proposition 5.20. ‣ 5.3. Interpretation as the count of colorings ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.20</span></a> respectively.
The last assertion follows from Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 3.5 (𝜔-duality). ‣ 3.2. Characteristic polynomial of a symmetric function ‣ 3. Characteristic polynomials of symmetric functions and representations ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>, (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In Example 3.14 (Permutation representations). ‣ 3.5. First examples ‣ 3. Characteristic polynomials of symmetric functions and representations ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.19</span></a>) and the fact that <math id="S5.SS3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is a permutation representation.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.22</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem22.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem22.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem21" title="Corollary 5.21. ‣ 5.3. Interpretation as the count of colorings ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.21</span></a>(1) can be viewed as a special case of Example <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem14" title="Example 3.14 (Permutation representations). ‣ 3.5. First examples ‣ 3. Characteristic polynomials of symmetric functions and representations ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.14</span></a> with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem22.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=\mathrm{Stab}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Stab</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=\mathrm{Stab}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, we present an independent proof above
using
Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem16" title="Lemma 5.16. ‣ 5.3. Interpretation as the count of colorings ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.16</span></a>, to provide a unified proof of (1) and (2) in Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem21" title="Corollary 5.21. ‣ 5.3. Interpretation as the count of colorings ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.21</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem23" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
