<div id="S6.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">When <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large, these are by Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6.6. ‣ 6.1. Asymptotic formulas ‣ 6. Asymptotic behavior and log-concavity ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a> equivalent to log-concavity of <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{d_{k}(k+1)^{n}/\binom{n-3}{k}\right\}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{d_{k}(k+1)^{n}/\binom{n-3}{k}\right\}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{c_{k}(k+1)^{n}/\binom{n-2}{k}\right\}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{c_{k}(k+1)^{n}/\binom{n-2}{k}\right\}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> at given <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. One can easily check that the latter are true for all <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This result generalizes the asymptotic ultra log-concavity of the Poincaré polynomial of <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\cal M}}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, previously discovered in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, which corresponds to the case of <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we examine the length log-concavity of the characteristic polynomials <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{P}_{n}(m,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{P}_{n}(m,t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n}(m,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n}(m,t)</annotation></semantics></math> in degree <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6.11</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="S6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for sufficiently large <math id="S6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;\left(\mathscr{P}_{n,k}^{n-j}\right)^{2}\geq\left(\mathscr{P}_{n,k}^{n-j-1}\right)\cdot\left(\mathscr{P}_{n,k}^{n-j+1}\right)~\text{ and}\\
&amp;\left(\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}^{n-j}\right)^{2}\geq\left(\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}^{n-j-1}\right)\cdot\left(\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}^{n-j+1}\right).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and</mtext></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;\left(\mathscr{P}_{n,k}^{n-j}\right)^{2}\geq\left(\mathscr{P}_{n,k}^{n-j-1}\right)\cdot\left(\mathscr{P}_{n,k}^{n-j+1}\right)~\text{ and}\\
&amp;\left(\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}^{n-j}\right)^{2}\geq\left(\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}^{n-j-1}\right)\cdot\left(\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}^{n-j+1}\right).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6.6. ‣ 6.1. Asymptotic formulas ‣ 6. Asymptotic behavior and log-concavity ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a>, when <math id="S6.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large, this reduces to the log-concavity of <math id="S6.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{c(n,n-j)\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{c(n,n-j)\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, which follows immediately from the identity <math id="S6.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="m(m+1)\cdots(m+n-1)=\sum_{j=0}^{n}c(n,j)m^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(m+1)\cdots(m+n-1)=\sum_{j=0}^{n}c(n,j)m^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 2.5. ‣ 2. Two-dimensional log-concavity ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Proofs of asymptotic formulas</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide proofs of Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1. ‣ 6.1. Asymptotic formulas ‣ 6. Asymptotic behavior and log-concavity ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6.6. ‣ 6.1. Asymptotic formulas ‣ 6. Asymptotic behavior and log-concavity ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a>. The proofs are based on the combinatorial description involving the weighted rooted trees. A central observation is that the dominant terms in both asymptotic formulas arise from the rooted tree with the maximum possible number of vertices, which accounts for the appearance of the Cayley numbers in the formula.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1. </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1. ‣ 6.1. Asymptotic formulas ‣ 6. Asymptotic behavior and log-concavity ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a></h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathscr{T}}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathscr{T}}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of isomorphism classes of weighted rooted trees with total weight <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and no inputs. Let</p>
<table id="S7.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{n}:\mathscr{T}_{n,k}\longrightarrow\overline{\mathscr{T}}_{k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{n}:\mathscr{T}_{n,k}\longrightarrow\overline{\mathscr{T}}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">be the forgetful map which forgets all the inputs of a given <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathscr{T}_{n,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathscr{T}_{n,k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In other words, the fiber of <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{T}\in\overline{\mathscr{T}}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{T}\in\overline{\mathscr{T}}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of all possible ways of attaching <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> inputs to the vertices of <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, subject to the valency conditions (cf. Definition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem4" title="Definition 5.4. ‣ 5.1. Recursive formulas for the representations ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Decompose <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}=\sum_{\overline{T}\in\overline{\mathscr{T}}_{k}}\mathscr{Q}_{n,\overline{T}},\quad\text{ where }\mathscr{Q}_{n,\overline{T}}:=\sum_{T\in F_{n}^{-1}(\overline{T})}\mathscr{S}_{U_{T}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒮</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}=\sum_{\overline{T}\in\overline{\mathscr{T}}_{k}}\mathscr{Q}_{n,\overline{T}},\quad\text{ where }\mathscr{Q}_{n,\overline{T}}:=\sum_{T\in F_{n}^{-1}(\overline{T})}\mathscr{S}_{U_{T}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Suppose that <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:=\lvert V(\overline{T})\rvert\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:=\lvert V(\overline{T})\rvert\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, by Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem21" title="Corollary 5.21. ‣ 5.3. Interpretation as the count of colorings ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.21</span></a>, <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n,\overline{T}}(m_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n,\overline{T}}(m_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is
bounded by the number of all possible ways of attaching <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> inputs to the vertices of <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and coloring them using <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> colors, which is given by</p>
<table id="S7.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}n_{1}+\cdots+n_{j}=n,\\
n_{i}\geq 0\end{subarray}}\prod_{i=1}^{j}\left(\kern-3.00003pt\left(\genfrac{}{}{0.0pt}{}{m_0}{n_i}\right)\kern-3.00003pt\right)=\left(\kern-3.00003pt\left(\genfrac{}{}{0.0pt}{}{jm_0}{n}\right)\kern-3.00003pt\right)=\frac{n^{jm_{0}-1}}{(jm_{0}-1)!}+O(n^{jm_{0}-2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0.0pt"><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mpadded style="width:0.089em;" width="0.089em"><mo>)</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0.0pt"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mpadded style="width:0.089em;" width="0.089em"><mo>)</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}n_{1}+\cdots+n_{j}=n,\\
n_{i}\geq 0\end{subarray}}\prod_{i=1}^{j}\left(\kern-3.00003pt\left(\genfrac{}{}{0.0pt}{}{m_0}{n_i}\right)\kern-3.00003pt\right)=\left(\kern-3.00003pt\left(\genfrac{}{}{0.0pt}{}{jm_0}{n}\right)\kern-3.00003pt\right)=\frac{n^{jm_{0}-1}}{(jm_{0}-1)!}+O(n^{jm_{0}-2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">In particular,
<math id="S7.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n,\overline{T}}(m)=O(n^{(k+1)m_{0}-2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n,\overline{T}}(m)=O(n^{(k+1)m_{0}-2})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{T}</annotation></semantics></math> with at most <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> vertices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert V(\overline{T})\rvert=k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert V(\overline{T})\rvert=k+1</annotation></semantics></math>. By a similar argument, we have</p>
<table id="S7.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n,\overline{T}}(m_{0})=\frac{1}{\lvert\Aut(\overline{T})\rvert}\cdot\frac{n^{(k+1)m_{0}-1}}{((k+1)m_{0}-1)!}+O(n^{(k+1)m_{0}-2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n,\overline{T}}(m_{0})=\frac{1}{\lvert\Aut(\overline{T})\rvert}\cdot\frac{n^{(k+1)m_{0}-1}}{((k+1)m_{0}-1)!}+O(n^{(k+1)m_{0}-2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, since <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximal possible number of vertices that <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{T}\in\overline{\mathscr{T}}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{T}\in\overline{\mathscr{T}}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> can have, every non-root vertex of <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{T}</annotation></semantics></math> has weight one while the root vertex has weight zero. In particular, automorphisms of <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{T}</annotation></semantics></math> as weighted rooted trees are precisely those as rooted trees. Therefore, by the same argument as in the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Theorem 6.3]</cite>, we have</p>
<table id="S7.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\overline{T}\in\overline{\mathscr{T}}_{k},~\lvert V(\overline{T})\rvert=k+1}\frac{1}{\lvert\Aut(\overline{T})\rvert}=\frac{(k+1)^{k-1}}{\lvert\mathbb{S}_{k}\rvert}=c_{k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.437em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\overline{T}\in\overline{\mathscr{T}}_{k},~\lvert V(\overline{T})\rvert=k+1}\frac{1}{\lvert\Aut(\overline{T})\rvert}=\frac{(k+1)^{k-1}}{\lvert\mathbb{S}_{k}\rvert}=c_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{T}</annotation></semantics></math> are considered as trees with <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, and <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k+1)^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k+1)^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of trees on <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> labeled vertices by Cayley’s tree formula.
Consequently,</p>
<table id="S7.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}(m_{0})=\sum_{\overline{T}\in\overline{T}_{k}}\mathscr{Q}_{n,\overline{T}}(m_{0})=c_{k}\cdot\frac{n^{(k+1)m_{0}-1}}{((k+1)m_{0}-1)!}+O(n^{(k+1)m_{0}-2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{Q}_{n,k}(m_{0})=\sum_{\overline{T}\in\overline{T}_{k}}\mathscr{Q}_{n,\overline{T}}(m_{0})=c_{k}\cdot\frac{n^{(k+1)m_{0}-1}}{((k+1)m_{0}-1)!}+O(n^{(k+1)m_{0}-2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">which implies the first formula.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the second formula, divide both sides of (<a href="#S5.E3" title="In 5.2. Recursive formulas for characteristic polynomials ‣ 5. Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} ‣ Characteristic polynomial of ℳ̄_{0,𝑛} and log-concavity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>) by <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{(k+1)m_{0}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{(k+1)m_{0}-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and take their limits as <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then,</p>
<table id="S7.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{\mathscr{P}_{n,k}(m_{0})+\mathscr{P}_{n,k-1}(m_{0})}{n^{(k+1)m_{0}-1}}=\frac{c_{k}}{((k+1)m_{0}-1)!}\\
